const articleSpecs = [
  { id: articleIds.disc, title: 'Lumbar disc prolapse, radicular pain and MRI-demonstrated protrusion', aliases: 'Family-163 disc compression\nDisc protrusion and radicular pain', micro: 'Disc prolapse and neural compression', concepts: conceptIds.disc, questions: 'Q-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-08\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-12\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-15', sources: `${source.assessment}\n${source.disc}`, codes: ['DISC-Q15'], related: articleIds.systemic, summary: 'This Draft article retains the existing Family-163 disc patterns and adds Q15 as a separate investigation-led occurrence.', body: 'An intervertebral disc contains an outer annulus fibrosus and an inner nucleus pulposus. Herniation can compress neural structures and produce radiating pain. Family-163 Q12 asks for the diagnosis, while Q15 repeats the vignette but asks for confirmation and keys MRI showing disc protrusion.\n\nThe Helwan cartilage lecture supports annulus disruption, nucleus-pulposus herniation and neural compression. It does not convert an MRI finding into a diagnosis. The exact Q15 lead-in is therefore preserved as a source-risk statement rather than rewritten.\n\nThe repeated vignette remains represented by separate question records because the assessment objectives and option sets differ. Q15 selects the imaging finding; Q12 selects lumbar prolapsed nucleus pulposus. Neither record is promoted beyond Draft. This objective-level distinction is material to the learner.\n\nDo not merge the two questions or repair the wording. Do not infer a different imaging modality. Publication requires independent medical verification and named faculty review.', conflicts: 'Q15 calls for a confirmatory diagnosis but keys an MRI finding; the wording and B key are preserved.', notes: 'This same-ID update retains Q8/Q12 links and adds Q15 only.' },
  { id: articleIds.tumour, title: 'Family-163 tumour patterns: plasma-cell, cartilage and bone-forming lesions', aliases: 'Family-163 tumour patterns\nMyeloma, chondrosarcoma and osteoma', micro: 'Tumour clinicopathologic patterns', concepts: `${conceptIds.myeloma}\nCON-MSK-3AD186B4605AF2\nCON-DER-78AF0815FE7330\nCON-MSK-A1148497BD0DB2\nCON-MSK-D11FA83681C5C6\n${conceptIds.chondrosarcoma}\n${conceptIds.osteoma}`, questions: 'Q-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-04\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-05\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-06\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-07\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-13\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-14\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-19\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-21\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-28', sources: `${source.assessment}\n${source.tumour}`, codes: ['TUMOURS-Q19-28', 'MYELOMA-Q21'], related: articleIds.systemic, summary: 'This Draft same-ID update retains the earlier tumour patterns and adds exact chondrosarcoma, repeated myeloma and osteoma occurrences.', body: 'Multiple myeloma is represented by lytic lesions, monoclonal protein and plasma-cell proliferation. Chondrosarcoma is represented by a destructive calcified chondroid mass with atypical chondrocytes in chondroid matrix. Osteoma is represented by a dense circumscribed mass composed of mature lamellar bone.\n\nQ19 uses spinal destruction, popcorn calcification, a bluish-white cut surface and atypical chondrocytes to key chondrosarcoma. Q21 repeats the myeloma diagnostic cluster but asks directly for the diagnosis. Q28 describes a hard forehead mass and prints osteoma after histology shows mature lamellar bone.\n\nThese items extend the existing article without erasing Q4-Q7 or Q13-Q14. Each keeps its exact wording and option order. The Q28 phrase “with revealed” remains unchanged because source fidelity controls.\n\nDo not substitute enchondroma for the malignant chondroid pattern. Do not merge Q21 with Q14’s prognostic objective. Do not rewrite Q28 grammar. All records require independent verification and faculty review.', conflicts: 'Q28 contains the literal grammatical phrase “with revealed”; no key-layer conflict occurs.', notes: 'This same-ID update retains every prior concept, question and evidence link before adding the new occurrences.' },
  { id: articleIds.systemic, title: 'Systemic bone disease patterns in Family-163: collagen, osteoporosis and Paget disease', aliases: 'Family-163 systemic bone disease\nOI, osteoporosis and Paget complications', micro: 'Systemic bone disorders', concepts: `${conceptIds.oi}\n${conceptIds.highTurnover}\n${conceptIds.osteoporosis}\n${conceptIds.paget}`, questions: 'Q-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-16\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-17\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-23\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-26\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-27', sources: `${source.assessment}\n${source.bms101}\n${source.bone}`, codes: ['OI-Q16', 'OSTEOPOROSIS-Q17-23', 'PAGET-Q26-27'], related: articleIds.joint, summary: 'This Draft article separates inherited collagen fragility, postmenopausal turnover, recurrent-fracture osteoporosis and Paget complications.', body: 'Osteogenesis imperfecta is linked in the local assessment to type I collagen and extensive fetal fractures. Osteoporosis is tested once as a postmenopausal high-turnover subtype and once as the diagnosis after recurrent fractures. Paget disease is recognised by thickened lamellae and irregular mosaic cement lines.\n\nQ16 prints Type I collagen. Q17 prints High-turnover Osteoporosis after low estrogen and vertebral microfractures, while Q23 prints Osteoporosis after recurrent long-bone fractures. These scopes remain separate so that the turnover subtype does not erase the broader bone-matrix and fragility concept.\n\nQ26 and Q27 share the Paget morphology but test different associated outcomes. Q26 prints Congestive heart failure and Q27 prints Secondary osteoarthritis. The exact Congestive heart failure wording is retained rather than silently converted into a more specific mechanism.\n\nDo not repair Q23’s switch from a female patient to “his diagnosis.” Do not collapse Q26 and Q27 into one question record. The local sources support curriculum context, not independent medical verification, so all content remains Draft.', conflicts: 'Q23 contains a pronoun mismatch. Q26’s Congestive heart failure wording remains literal and source-bound.', notes: 'No written, practical or media record is inferred from these MCQs.' },
  { id: articleIds.joint, title: 'Family-163 gout, osteoarthritis and carpal-tunnel association patterns', aliases: 'Family-163 gout and OA\nCarpal tunnel association exception', micro: 'Metabolic and degenerative joint patterns', concepts: `${conceptIds.gout}\n${conceptIds.carpal}\n${conceptIds.oa}`, questions: 'Q-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-18\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-22\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-24\nQ-HU-LCS103-MSK-F163-25', sources: `${source.assessment}\n${source.joint}\n${source.bms101}`, codes: ['GOUT-Q18-22', 'CARPAL-Q24', 'OA-Q25'], related: articleIds.systemic, summary: 'This Draft article preserves two gout objectives, a source-bound carpal-tunnel exception set and a classic osteoarthritis pattern.', body: 'Gout is represented by recurrent podagra, chalky material and negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals. Osteoarthritis is represented by activity-related pain, osteophytes and joint-space narrowing. Q24 is a separate association-exception item for carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nQ18 prints Leukemia as the oncology association, but the available Helwan joint lecture independently supports only urate and hyperuricaemia context. Q22 prints a serum uric acid of 15.7 mg/dL and retains the exact “micro gm/dL” unit wording elsewhere in the option set. The leukemia relation therefore remains explicitly source-bound.\n\nQ24 prints Diabetes insipidus as the condition not associated with carpal tunnel syndrome. The HU-BMS-101 material supports local carpal-tunnel anatomy, not the complete systemic association set. Q25 separately prints Osteoarthritis for activity-related spine and hip pain with osteophytes and narrowed joint space.\n\nDo not promote Q18 or Q24 into independently verified teaching. Do not change any laboratory units. Keep every question Draft until independent medical verification and named Helwan faculty review occur.', conflicts: 'The leukemia association and complete carpal-tunnel association set lack independent Helwan teaching confirmation.', notes: 'The source-bound limitations are repeated in concepts, evidence and question notes.' },
]
